## What Happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native cryptographic libraries (Security.framework / CryptoKit). On macOS, .NET's `DSA.Create()` relies on the platform crypto provider, so it now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. Windows and Linux are unaffected because their crypto backends (CNG and OpenSSL, respectively) still support DSA.

This is part of an ongoing .NET trend of surfacing platform deprecations — the same removal was signaled in earlier previews and is now enforced.

## How to Fix

**Option A — Use a managed DSA implementation (recommended if you must keep DSA):**

Use **BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle` or `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` on NuGet) which provides a pure-managed DSA verifier that works on all platforms:

```csharp
// Example: verify with BouncyCastle
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option B — Migrate to a modern algorithm:**

If you control both signing and verification, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported natively on all three platforms in .NET 11.

**Option C — Force OpenSSL on macOS:**

Install OpenSSL via Homebrew and set the `CLR_OPENSSL_VERSION_OVERRIDE` environment variable so .NET uses OpenSSL instead of the native Apple backend. This is fragile and not recommended for production.

---

**TL;DR:** Apple dropped DSA → .NET surfaces that as `PlatformNotSupportedException`. Use BouncyCastle for a cross-platform managed implementation, or migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519.
